description:
Read a classic pcap capture file (lesson 4.01): its global header and its packet records, in either byte order.

```cpp
struct PcapInfo { bool bigEndian; bool nanoseconds; std::uint32_t snapLength; std::uint32_t linkType; };
struct PcapPacket { std::uint32_t seconds; std::uint32_t fraction; std::uint32_t originalLength; std::vector<std::uint8_t> data; };

std::optional<PcapInfo> readHeader(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& file);
std::optional<std::vector<PcapPacket>> readPackets(const std::vector<std::uint8_t>& file);
```

- **readHeader** needs at least 24 bytes. Read the first 4 bytes as a little-endian number: `0xA1B2C3D4` is a little-endian file with microsecond timestamps, `0xA1B23C4D` little-endian with nanoseconds, `0xD4C3B2A1` big-endian with microseconds and `0x4D3CB2A1` big-endian with nanoseconds. Any other magic fails. The major version (bytes 4-5, in the file's byte order) must be 2. Then fill in the snapshot length (bytes 16-19) and the link type (bytes 20-23).
- **readPackets** reads the header, then every record up to the end of the file: a 16-byte record header (seconds, fraction of a second, captured length, original length, all in the file's byte order) and `captured length` bytes of data. `fraction` is the microseconds or nanoseconds field as stored. It fails if the header is bad, a record header or its data runs past the end of the file, or the captured length is greater than the snapshot length or the original length. A file with no records gives an empty list.

